    <!-- Guide / first launch -->
    <string name="guide_title">How ResQ works</string>
    <string name="guide_first_title">Before you start</string>
    <string name="guide_intro_sends_title">What ResQ sends</string>
    <string name="guide_intro_sends">When you press Start, your phone broadcasts your location and your message over Bluetooth to anyone nearby who has ResQ. It is not encrypted, so anyone close enough can read it.</string>
    <string name="guide_intro_range_title">What it cannot do</string>
    <string name="guide_intro_range">ResQ only works if another phone with ResQ is within Bluetooth range, and it does not contact emergency services. If you can, call your local emergency number as well.</string>
    <string name="guide_steps_title">Three steps</string>
    <string name="guide_step_1">Allow Bluetooth, location and notifications when asked.</string>
    <string name="guide_step_2">Add where you are: Settings → My location.</string>
    <string name="guide_step_3">Press Start on the Send screen. Keep the phone on; ResQ keeps broadcasting in the background.</string>
    <string name="guide_help_title">If you need help</string>
    <string name="guide_help_1">Press Start. Your SOS goes out at once, even before GPS has a position. A fresh position follows as soon as there is one.</string>
    <string name="guide_help_2">Tap one of the cards that appear, or type a short message. The change reaches others after a moment.</string>
    <string name="guide_help_3">Press Stop only when you are safe. Nearby phones are told you stopped, and show it as unverified.</string>
    <string name="guide_help_4">If the app is closed by the phone, ResQ brings an SOS from the last 12 hours back when it restarts or when you open it.</string>
    <string name="guide_helper_title">If you want to help</string>
    <string name="guide_helper_1">Open the Scan screen and press Start scan. Your phone listens for SOS from nearby phones and passes them on to others, so they travel further.</string>
    <string name="guide_helper_2">Each card shows the message, the position and how long ago it was heard. Copy puts the coordinates on the clipboard for a map app.</string>
    <string name="guide_helper_3">Signal lost means nothing has been heard from them for a while. The position shown is the last one known.</string>
    <string name="guide_hops_title">Reading “relayed ×N”</string>
    <string name="guide_hops">No label means your phone heard the sender directly, so they are within Bluetooth range, tens of metres. relayed ×1 means one other phone passed it on, relayed ×2 means two, and so on. A higher number means the sender may be further away and the copy is more indirect. Use the position and its age to find them.</string>
    <string name="guide_ready_title">Get ready before an emergency</string>
    <string name="guide_ready_1">Set My location in Settings, such as your room, floor or building.</string>
    <string name="guide_ready_2">Allow all permissions, and turn Location on.</string>
    <string name="guide_ready_3">Turn off battery optimisation for ResQ in your phone settings, so Android does not stop it.</string>
    <string name="guide_ready_4">Keep some battery. Battery saver in Settings sends less often to make it last.</string>
